    routeSummary: "Siem Reap → Angkor small circuit → Tonle Sap → Banteay Srei and the far temples",
    intro: "The fourth day is the one that changes the trip. Three days in Siem Reap is Angkor; four is Angkor plus the thing Angkor was built on — water. The Khmer empire ran on the Tonle Sap and on a system of reservoirs and canals that fed a city of several hundred thousand people, and you cannot really read the temples without seeing the lake they were fed from.\n\nSo this route still stays in one place, and the extra day goes to the lake and the countryside rather than to a fourth set of ruins. That is also the honest answer to temple fatigue: by the second straight day of stone most people have stopped looking. The three-day Angkor pass, valid on any three days inside a ten-day window, lets you break the temple days up around the lake day at no extra cost.\n\nFour days is also enough to stop eating at the places with laminated photo menus. Khmer food is milder than Thai and sourer than Vietnamese, built on prahok fermented fish paste and on kroeung, a lemongrass, galangal, turmeric and kaffir-lime paste pounded fresh every morning, and it is worth one deliberate evening rather than four accidental ones.",
    bestFor: "A first visit with breathing room, travellers who dislike back-to-back sightseeing days, and anyone arriving on an afternoon flight who does not want to lose a temple day to it.",
    quickAnswer: "Four days is still Siem Reap: two temple days on the small circuit and the far temples, one on the Tonle Sap, and one lost to the 45-kilometre run in from the new airport. The lake day is the one to protect — four straight days of stone is where most people stop seeing anything.",
