        heading: "The Tonle Sap, and a day off from stone",
        narrative: "This is the day the pass rule pays for itself. Leave the temples alone and go out to the lake instead; your three-day pass is untouched and still valid for tomorrow.\n\nThe Tonle Sap is the largest freshwater lake in Southeast Asia and the only one in the world that reverses twice a year. In the monsoon the Mekong pushes water back up the Tonle Sap river and the lake swells several times its dry-season size; from about November it drains again. The villages live on that rhythm. Kampong Phluk's houses stand on stilts six to ten metres high — waterline in October, absurd stick-legs in March. Kampong Khleang is bigger, further out and far less visited. Chong Kneas is the closest to town and the most worked-over by tourism; if a price looks too cheap, that is usually where it is going.\n\nWhich village works, and whether a boat can reach the flooded forest at all, depends entirely on the month, so ask before booking rather than after. In the dry months the honest alternative is the countryside: sugar palms, rice fields in whatever state the year has them, and villages five minutes off the temple road that almost nobody on the circuit sees.",
